Sure! Let's break down the task into smaller steps. We'll create an HTML structure for a card, and then we'll style it with CSS to make it rotate when clicked and have a small rotation effect on hover.

### Step 1: HTML Structure

First, let's set up the basic HTML structure for our card:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Card Rotation</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
    <div class="card" id="myCard">
        <p>Click me to flip!</p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
```

### Step 2: CSS Styling

Now, let's add the CSS to style the card and implement the rotation effects. We'll use CSS transitions and transforms to achieve this.

```css
/* styles.css */

.card {
    width: 200px;
    height: 200px;
    perspective: 1000px; /* This gives the 3D effect */
    transition: transform 0.5s; /* Smooth transition for hover */
}

.card.flipped {
    transform: rotateY(180deg); /* Rotate the card when flipped */
}

.card p {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    text-align: center;
    background-color: white;
    padding: 20px;
    box-shadow: 0 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
}

.card:hover {
    transform: rotateY(15deg); /* Small rotation on hover */
}
```

### Step 3: JavaScript for Click Event

Finally, we need to add some JavaScript to handle the click event and toggle the `flipped` class.

```javascript
// script.js
document.getElementById('myCard').addEventListener('click', function() {
    this.classList.toggle('flipped'); // Toggle the 'flipped' class on click
});
```

