### Concept Explanation

The key concept here is creating a generic function using templates in C++. A template allows you to write functions or classes that can work with multiple data types. In this case, you want to create a function that compares two values of the same type and returns the larger one.

Since you are using the less-than (`<`) operator, the function will only work with types that support comparison via `<`. For example, integers, floating-point numbers, and strings all support `<`, but pointers do not.

### Example Code

Here is a simple example that demonstrates how to define such a template function:

```cpp
// Function template declaration with a comment explaining the type requirement
template <typename T>
T maximum(T a, T b) {
    // This function returns the larger of two values of type T.
    // It uses the < operator, so T must be a type that supports comparison.
    return (a < b) ? b : a;
}

int main() {
    int x = 10;
    int y = 20;
    std::cout << "Maximum of " << x << " and " << y << " is " << maximum(x, y) << std::endl;

    float a = 3.5f;
    float b = 2.8f;
    std::cout << "Maximum of " << a << " and " << b << " is " << maximum(a, b) << std::endl;

    std::string s1 = "apple";
    std::string s2 = "banana";
    std::cout << "Maximum of " << s1 << " and " << s2 << " is " << maximum(s1, s2) << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

